names></name><name><surname>Hanawa</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Distinct genomic integration of MLV and SIV vectors in primate hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells.</article-title><source>PLoS Biol</source><volume>2</volume><fpage>e423</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15550989</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Fears1"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fears</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Mathieu</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Zeleznik-Le</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Huang</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Rowley</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1996</year><article-title>Intergenic splicing of MDS1 and EVI1 occurs in normal tissues as well as in myeloid leukemia and produces a new member of the PR domain family.</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A</source><volume>93</volume><fpage>1642</fpage><lpage>1647</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8643684</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Baum1"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Baum</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Dullmann</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Fehse</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Meyer</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Side effects of retroviral gene transfer into hematopoietic stem cells.</article-title><source>Blood</source><volume>101</volume><fpage>2099</fpage><lpage>2114</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12511419</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Metais1"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Metais</surname><given-names>JY</given-names></name><name><surname>Dunbar</surname><given-names>CE</given-names></name></person-group><year>2008</year><article-title>The MDS1-EVI1 gene complex as a retrovirus integration site: impact on behavior of hematopoietic cells and implications for gene therapy.</article-title><source>Mol Ther</source><volume>16</volume><fpage>439</fpage><lpage>449</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18227842</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Senchenko1"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Senchenko</surname><given-names>VN</given-names></name><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Loginov</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Bazov</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Angeloni</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Discovery of frequent homozygous deletions in chromosome 3p21.3 LUCA and AP20 regions in renal, lung and breast carcinomas.</article-title><source>Oncogene</source><volume>23</volume><fpage>5719</fpage><lpage>5728</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15208675</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Calin1"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Calin</surname><given-names>GA</given-names></name><name><surname>Trapasso</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Shimizu</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Dumitru</surname><given-names>CD</given-names></name><name><surname>Yendamuri</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Familial cancer associated with a polymorphism in ARLTS1.</article-title><source>N Engl J Med</source><volume>352</volume><fpage>1667</fpage><lpage>1676</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15843669</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Suzuki1"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Suzuki</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Shen</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Akagi</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Morse</surname><given-names>HC</given-names></name><name><surname>Malley</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>New genes involved in cancer identified by retroviral tagging.</article-title><source>Nat Genet</source><volume>32</volume><fpage>166</fpage><lpage>174</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12185365</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Christensen1"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Christensen</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Ambartsumian</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Gilestro</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Thomsen</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Comoglio</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Proteolytic processing converts the repelling signal Sema3E into an inducer of invasive growth and lung metastasis.</article-title><source>Cancer Res</source><volume>65</volume><fpage>6167</fpage><lpage>6177</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16024618</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Mitchell1"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mitchell</surname><given-names>RS</given-names></name><name><surname>Beitzel</surname><given-names>BF</given-names></name><name><surname>Schroder</surname><given-names>AR</given-names></name><name><surname>Shinn</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Retroviral DNA integration: ASLV, HIV, and MLV show distinct target site preferences.</article-title><source>PLoS Biol</source><volume>2</volume><fpage>E234</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15314653</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Wu1"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wu</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Crise</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Burgess</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Transcription start regions in the human genome are favored targets for MLV integration.</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>300</volume><fpage>1749</fpage><lpage>1751</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12805549</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Schwarzwaelder1"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Schwarzwaelder</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Howe</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Schmidt</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Brugman</surname><given-names>MH</given-names></name><name><surname>Deichmann</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Gammaretrovirus-mediated correction of SCID-X1 is associated with skewed vector integration site distribution in vivo.</article-title><source>J Clin Invest</source><volume>117</volume><fpage>2241</fpage><lpage>2249</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17671654</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004211-Aiuti1"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Aiuti</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name>